\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Covariance is inverse precision}
\date{2026-10-16}

\p{For a \vocabk{positive precision parameter}{fdip-0004} #{Q} on
#{\mathbb R^V}, use its [counting array](fdip-002O). The centered
\vocabk{Gaussian probability law}{fdip-0005} satisfies
##{\langle\phi_v\phi_w\rangle_Q=(Q^{-1})_{vw}.}
The left side is the coordinate
\vocabk{covariance}{fdip-0006}, since the coordinate means vanish.
This is the precision form of
\citet{Proposition B.55, p. 505, and Theorem 8.4, p. 385}{friedli2017statistical}.
For #{Q=Q_m=L+m^2I}, it identifies the covariance with the
\vocabk{Green kernel}{fdip-000A}.}

\proof{
\p{Write #{g_a(x)=\exp(-a x^2/2)} and
#{I(a)=\int_{\mathbb R}g_a(x)\,dx} for #{a>0}.
The [normalization argument](fdip-0005) gives #{0<I(a)<\infty}.
The [polynomial bound](fdip-0006) proves absolute integrability of
#{xg_a(x)} and #{x^2g_a(x)}. Oddness therefore gives
#{\int_{\mathbb R}xg_a(x)\,dx=0}. On #{[-R,R]}, integration of
#{(xg_a(x))'=g_a(x)-a x^2g_a(x)} yields
##{\begin{aligned}
&a\int_{-R}^R x^2g_a(x)\,dx\\
&\quad=\int_{-R}^R g_a(x)\,dx\\
&\qquad-2R\exp(-aR^2/2).
\end{aligned}}
The same polynomial bound gives
#{R\exp(-aR^2/2)\le C\exp(-aR^2/4)} for #{R\ge0}, with a finite
constant #{C}; hence the boundary term tends to zero. Monotone
convergence on both nonnegative integrands now gives
##{\frac{1}{I(a)}\int_{\mathbb R}x^2g_a(x)\,dx=a^{-1}.}
Thus the integration by parts has an integrable second moment and a
vanishing boundary term.}

\p{Diagonalize #{Q=U\operatorname{diag}(q_1,\ldots,q_n)U^T}, with
#{U} orthogonal and every #{q_i>0}. Under #{\phi=Uy}, the normalized
density becomes
##{\prod_{i=1}^n\frac{g_{q_i}(y_i)}{I(q_i)}.}
All coordinate products are absolutely integrable by
\ref{fdip-0006}, so Fubini applies. Integrating the other coordinates
gives one. For distinct #{i,j}, the two remaining first-moment integrals
vanish; for #{i=j}, the preceding calculation gives
##{\langle y_i y_j\rangle_Q=\delta_{ij}q_i^{-1}.}
Expanding the finite sums #{\phi_v=\sum_iU_{vi}y_i} therefore gives
##{\begin{aligned}
\langle\phi_v\phi_w\rangle_Q
  &=\sum_i U_{vi}q_i^{-1}U_{wi}\\
  &=(Q^{-1})_{vw}.
\end{aligned}}
Here expectations in #{y} denote the same law after the indicated change
of coordinates.}
}

\p{The [alternating four-cycle](fdip-0003) makes the formula explicit.
For #{t,m>0}, the [matrix #{L_t}](fdip-0008) has the following
orthonormal eigenvectors #{u_a}, written as rows #{2u_a^T}:
##{\begin{array}{c|c|c}
a&2u_a^T&\lambda_a\\\hline
0&(1,1,1,1)&0\\
1&(1,-1,-1,1)&2\\
2&(1,1,-1,-1)&2t\\
3&(1,-1,1,-1)&2+2t
\end{array}}}
\p{Each displayed row has squared norm four, distinct rows have dot
product zero, and multiplication by the displayed #{L_t} gives the stated
eigenvalue. For example its first row sends
#{(1,-1,-1,1)^T} to #{(1+t)+1-t=2}; the remaining coordinates give
#{2(-1,-1,1)}. The same substitution verifies the other three rows.
These vectors still form an orthonormal basis when #{t=1}, although two
eigenvalues then agree. With #{q_a=m^2+\lambda_a},
##{\begin{aligned}
G_m&=\sum_{a=0}^3q_a^{-1}u_au_a^T,\\
\det Q_{t,m}&=m^2(m^2+2)(m^2+2t)\\
 &\quad\cdot(m^2+2+2t).
\end{aligned}}
The [partition function](fdip-0005) is consequently
##{Z_{t,m}=\frac{(2\pi)^2}{\sqrt{q_0q_1q_2q_3}}.}
Two covariance entries, read from the signs in the table, are
##{\begin{aligned}
4\langle\phi_0^2\rangle_{t,m}
  &=q_0^{-1}+q_1^{-1}\\
  &\quad+q_2^{-1}+q_3^{-1},\\
4\langle\phi_0\phi_1\rangle_{t,m}
  &=q_0^{-1}-q_1^{-1}\\
  &\quad+q_2^{-1}-q_3^{-1}.
\end{aligned}}
The sign of each term in the second entry is the product of the first
two coordinates of its eigenvector.}

\p{Mathlib's
\leanref{ProbabilityTheory.covariance_eval_multivariateGaussian}
identifies coordinate covariance with the supplied positive semidefinite
covariance matrix #{S}. TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.Probability.multivariateGaussian_eq_withDensity}
gives the Lebesgue density when #{S} is positive definite. For this
precision convention, one must take #{S=Q^{-1}} and identify its density,
determinant factor and Euclidean coordinates with #{\mu_Q}; the theorem
does not take #{Q} as a precision argument. The integral proof above
establishes the stated identity directly.}
